00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:44.000
Now, we finally come to this&nbsp;
video’s topic: How do GANs work?

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:49.840
There are a lot of GAN types and you&nbsp;
have seen some in action: StyleGan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:54.720
Pix2Pix, and so on and so on,&nbsp;
but what do all have in common?

00:03:55.360 --> 00:04:01.200
The original GAN model architecture: it was&nbsp;
introduced first in 2014 in the paper of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:05.040
Ian Goodfellow and collaborators&nbsp;
linked in the description below.

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:08.640
Wow, 2014! That was ages ago!

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:15.840
A GAN is based on two neural networks working&nbsp;
against each other, thus the name adversarial:

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:21.520
What’s cool about a GAN architecture is that&nbsp;
it can generate data (it’s in the name),

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:24.640
but also do so in a&nbsp;
self-supervised learning setting.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:29.200
This means that the data is not&nbsp;
annotated and that the model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:32.080
creates its own annotations and learns from them.

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:36.800
This is what the discriminator, one&nbsp;
of the two neural networks, does.

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:41.600
Now we will see how:
The only thing we need is raw data, like bare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:47.120
text if we want to do Natural Language Processing&nbsp;
or just images if we want to do Compute Vision.

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:52.080
These samples, we call real, because&nbsp;
these images come from real data.

00:04:52.720 --> 00:05:00.080
But for GANs, we also need some fake data, that&nbsp;
is coming from somewhere else. Hmm… mysterious.

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:02.880
For now let’s bring the&nbsp;
discriminator onto the stage.

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:06.640
This is a neural network with&nbsp;
an architecture of your choice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:11.680
it can be just fully connected layers with a&nbsp;
sigmoid for binary classification at the end.

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:17.760
This discriminator has to predict whether the&nbsp;
image that is being passed to it as input is real.

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:20.160
Or if it is fake.

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:24.080
So if it is coming from the other set of data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:27.280
that we did not explain&nbsp;
exactly where it comes from.

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:33.600
Well, this data comes from the second&nbsp;
neural network, called the generator.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:36.000
(strange voice) Player 2 joined the game.

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:40.400
The generator is also a&nbsp;
neural network of your choice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:44.960
because however it looks like, it&nbsp;
has to generate these fake samples.

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:47.840
Where does it generate these fake samples from?

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:55.440
Well, from scratch! This is in many cases just&nbsp;
some noise variables in this so called latent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:58.960
space, that the generator produces samples from.

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:05.840
But how? If we remember how neural networks&nbsp;
work, we know how much is in their parameters.

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:12.560
The generator takes one number from this&nbsp;
latent space, multiplies it with a parameter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:16.400
sums it up with another number&nbsp;
multiplied by another parameter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:22.080
passes it through a non-linear activation&nbsp;
function and repeats this for all layers.

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:28.240
And to put it almost anecdotally: Let’s&nbsp;
say we want to generate a picture of a cat.

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:34.000
The generator learns the right parameters&nbsp;
to multiply the latent space with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:36.880
in order to generate grass&nbsp;
pixels for the background.

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:42.240
The grass pixels are nothing but RGB&nbsp;
values, so for the green channel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:49.520
we want a value as close as possible to 255&nbsp;
and zeroes in the red and blue channels.

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:52.800
This would be the ideal case, but of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:55.840
at the beginning it would&nbsp;
generate only noise from noise.

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:00.640
So how does the generator&nbsp;
learn to produce grass or cats?

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:04.160
It has to take feedback from&nbsp;
somewhere on how well it does!

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:09.200
Here is where the game starts between&nbsp;
the discriminator and the generator.

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:12.320
The discriminator’s job is apparently easy:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:17.600
now knowing from which image pool the input is&nbsp;
coming from, it has to predict if the image is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:20.880
coming from the real data
or from the Generator.

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:26.640
If it succeeds to tell pictures&nbsp;
apart, the discriminator has won.

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:32.880
If the discriminator predicts an image&nbsp;
coming from the generator as being real,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:35.520
then it has lost and the generator has won.

00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:40.000
This is why this is a non-cooperative&nbsp;
game the two are playing.

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:46.080
Depending on the errors the discriminator&nbsp;
makes, the parameters of the discriminator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:49.120
are updated in order to perform better next time.

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:53.520
At the beginning one would&nbsp;
guess that his task is easy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:58.000
because the generator’s garbage is not&nbsp;
hard to tell apart from real pictures.

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:01.920
But every time that the discriminator&nbsp;
gives a verdict for a picture of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:06.640
the generator to be real or fake,
the generator uses this feedback in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:10.800
backpropagation to update it’s weights&nbsp;
and perform even better next time.

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:17.440
And this alternating training of the generator&nbsp;
and discriminator in their game continues,&nbsp;

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:21.600
until the generator is producing&nbsp;
images of such quality,&nbsp;

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:24.320
that the discriminator has no other chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:28.720
than to randomly guess
if the images are fake or real.

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:30.880
Or at least, this is the ideal case.

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:36.640
Because in practice, GANs are notoriously&nbsp;
difficult to train to convergence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:39.840
It is not impossible, but very hard.

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:43.840
It only works, if the discriminator&nbsp;
and the generator learn together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:46.320
and grow in competence at the same rate.

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:52.880
If one becomes far too good for the other&nbsp;
at any time, then it is like playing chess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:58.320
against your grandpa. He always wins by a&nbsp;
high margin and you have no idea how or why.

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:05.040
One of the most common problems is called mode&nbsp;
collapse. This happens when the generator maps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:11.680
the latent space to the same output, producing&nbsp;
high quality outputs with very low diversity.

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:17.760
Another thing that makes the convergence&nbsp;
harder is that the Nash equilibrium and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:22.560
with it convergence is not guaranteed.&nbsp;
[disk scratch] Whaaat? In English please?

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:28.000
We remember, this is a non-cooperative game&nbsp;
that the discriminator and the generator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:31.120
are playing, since they want to fool each other.

00:09:31.120 --> 00:09:37.200
The win of one side is the loss of the other.&nbsp;
The Nash equilibrium is a game theoretical term&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:40.640
named after the mathematician John Forbes Nash Jr.

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:44.720
for describing a stable state of&nbsp;
the interaction of the participants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:49.840
in which no participant can make gains&nbsp;
by changing only their own strategy.

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:54.960
And this is exactly what is not&nbsp;
guaranteed in this non-cooperative game.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.960 --> 00:10:01.520
It suffices if only the generator changes strategy&nbsp;
and starts to produce suddenly perfect images,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:04.800
because then discriminator&nbsp;
has no more chance to recover.

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:08.720
It will think the stunningly generated images&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:12.320
are real and all feedback it&nbsp;
will start to get is negative.

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:17.440
Conversely, if the discriminator&nbsp;
becomes very good, the generator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:22.560
will also get only negative feedback, with&nbsp;
no chance of finding back the right track.

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:29.280
And to combat these and also other problems,&nbsp;
this whole GAN zoo was established.

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:32.880
GANs have been amazingly&nbsp;
applied in computer vision.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:36.400
What about other fields, like&nbsp;
Natural Language Processing?

00:10:36.400 --> 00:10:40.000
Well there, some more tricks&nbsp;
are required for training GANs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:47.600
because unlike image data that takes values&nbsp;
from 0 to 255, text data is not continuous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:52.880
and neural networks don’t like that and&nbsp;
difficult workarounds have to be found.

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:56.560
So while GANs are also sometimes used NLP,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.560 --> 00:11:00.640
there will be no amazing examples for&nbsp;
this like the ones in the beginning.

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:04.080
For NLP and GANs, it was just not meant to be,&nbsp;

00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:07.520
since all NLP attention is&nbsp;
going towards the Transformer

00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:10.400
(haha, attention. pun intended).

00:11:10.400 --> 00:11:16.720
But hope is not lost, since the next amazing&nbsp;
GAN application in NLP could be done by you!

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:21.200
Let us know in the comments if you would&nbsp;
like to see a video with the math behind GANs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:25.360
or let us know about any other topics&nbsp;
you would like to have explained simply!

00:11:25.360 --> 00:11:27.840
Okay bye!

